To get started creating an MCP server, create an `mcp.Server` instance, add
features to it, and then run it over an `mcp.Transport`. For example, this
server adds a single simple tool, and then connects it to clients over
stdin/stdout:
```go
package main
import (
	"context"
	"log"
	"github.com/modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk/mcp"
)
type Input struct {
	Name string `json:"name" jsonschema:"the name of the person to greet"`
}
type Output struct {
	Greeting string `json:"greeting" jsonschema:"the greeting to tell to the user"`
}

### Step 1: Add to your AI tool
Add to your MCP configuration (see client-specific setup below):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "spec-workflow": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@pimzino/spec-workflow-mcp@latest", "/path/to/your/project"]
    }
  }
}
```

Add the following config to your MCP client:
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "chrome-devtools": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest"]
    }
  }
}
```
> [!NOTE]
> Using `chrome-devtools-mcp@latest` ensures that your MCP client will always use the latest version of the Chrome DevTools MCP server.
